/*
  Solution idea:
  Build a graph on positions 1..N plus one segment-tree-like auxiliary structure.

  Each operation adds a complete graph on interval [L, R] with edge cost C.
  We can model this using two segment trees (out-tree and in-tree) and one extra node per operation.

  For an operation [L, R], cost C:
    - from every vertex u in [L, R], we can go to op-node with cost C
    - from op-node, we can go to every vertex v in [L, R] with cost 0
  Then any u -> op -> v path costs C, exactly as required.
  Also u -> u via the op-node is possible with cost C, but since all costs are nonnegative, this does not affect shortest paths.

  To connect a single vertex / interval to many nodes efficiently:
    - out-tree: node -> children edges cost 0, leaves correspond to original vertices.
      To connect op-node -> all vertices in [L,R], add edges op -> coverNodes(out-tree) cost 0.
    - in-tree: children -> parent edges cost 0, leaves correspond to original vertices.
      To connect all vertices in [L,R] -> op-node with cost C, add edges coverNodes(in-tree) -> op cost C.

  Total nodes/edges are O((N+M) log N), then run Dijkstra from leaf(1) to leaf(N).

  Complexity: O((N + M) log N log (N + M)) due to Dijkstra on O((N+M)logN) graph.
*/

